-- Geocentric ephemeris — the apparent positions of the Sun and Mars on the sky,
-- computed from Keplerian orbital elements for 2027 February 20.0 TT
-- (JD 2461456.5), the date of a Mars opposition.
--
-- This is the first example in the tree to do floating-point work.  It exercises
-- the whole float surface — sin, cos, sqrt, atan2, asin, floor, round — and
-- it is deliberately a *program* rather than a feature demonstration: nine stages
-- from a calendar date to a formatted right ascension, each one a stage that can
-- be wrong on its own.
--
-- =============================================================================
-- WHY THIS FILE IS DECOMPOSED THE WAY IT IS
-- =============================================================================
--
-- Vera references bindings by type and position (`@T.n`), not by name, so the
-- shape of a program's *types* decides how legible its slot references are.
-- Dense numerical code is the hard case: write it all in bare `@Float64` and a
-- five-parameter function carries `@Float64.0` through `@Float64.4`, where the
-- rightmost parameter is index 0 and every new `let` of that type shifts all the
-- others up by one.  That is the shape SKILL.md's "Keep functions small" section
-- warns about, and the shape that makes the commutative-operations trap bite:
-- swapping two indices is invisible for `+` and `*` and silently wrong for `-`,
-- `/`, and comparisons.
--
-- So this file gives every physical quantity its own type.  A type alias gets its
-- own slot stack — DE_BRUIJN.md §6 is explicit that `type Cnt = Int;` does not
-- make `@Cnt` and `@Int` interchangeable, and a parameter declared `@Cnt` is
-- reached only by `@Cnt.0`.  Distinct names therefore *partition* the index
-- space.  The payoff is visible in `rot_x` below: five parameters, five separate
-- stacks, every reference at `.0`, and no index left to get wrong.  Run
-- `vera check --explain-slots examples/ephemeris.vera` to see the resolution
-- table the compiler builds.
--
-- The same trick does the real work inside `Vec3` and `Elements`.  A `Vec3`
-- holding three `Float64` fields would bind three same-typed slots on every
-- destructure, with the *last* field at index 0 — precisely the ordering mistake
-- that turns a coordinate difference into nonsense.  Holding `XAu`, `YAu`, `ZAu`
-- instead, each component destructures to its own `.0`.
--
-- Two aliases are refinement types rather than plain ones, because there the
-- bound is load-bearing: `Ecc` states an orbit is elliptical, and `wrap_deg`
-- returns a `Deg` the verifier has *proved* lies in [0, 360).
--
-- Where a single function still needs several bindings of one type, the second
-- documented tool is a `where` helper: DE_BRUIJN.md calls it "a closed,
-- parameter-rooted scope, the one binding form that does not nest", so it starts
-- a fresh index space and reaching a parent binding from inside one is E130
-- rather than a silent capture.  This file mostly does not need them, because
-- typing the quantities separately removed the collisions first.  That ordering
-- is the point: decompose by *meaning* and the slot discipline follows.
--
-- Further reading: SKILL.md ("Keep functions small", "Iteration"), DE_BRUIJN.md
-- (the full slot-resolution rules), spec/03-slot-references.md, and
-- spec/06-contracts.md for the verification tiers referred to below.
--
-- =============================================================================
-- WHAT VERIFIES, AND WHAT DOES NOT
-- =============================================================================
--
-- Of 49 proof obligations, 47 discharge at Tier 1 and 2 fall to Tier 3.  Unlike
-- every other example in the tree, this one does not verify uniformly -- and the
-- split is the most interesting thing in the file.
--
-- The two that do not are categorical, not marginal: no solver budget reaches
-- them, and nothing about this file's arrangement could.  That distinction is
-- worth the paragraph it costs, because the first draft of this program did not
-- have it.  The `Ecc` bound used to be inherited from refined `Julian` and
-- `Century` types -- saying "this date is inside the element set's window" in
-- the type, and letting the verifier carry that through a division chain to the
-- eccentricity.  It worked, and it cost about nine to eleven seconds against a
-- ten-second default budget, which meant the reported tier depended on how busy
-- the machine was.  A verification result that changes with the load average is
-- not a verification result.
--
-- So the bound is established where the value is built instead.  `earth_elements`
-- and `mars_elements` guard the eccentricity with exactly the `Ecc` predicate,
-- so the then-branch discharges by path condition and the else-branch by
-- constant folding -- three comparisons rather than a division chain, and the
-- whole file verifies in under a second.  `Julian` and `Century` are plain
-- aliases again, which is the honest shape: the date types were carrying a
-- proof obligation about eccentricity, which was never really their business.
--
-- The arithmetic spine proves: Julian-day arithmetic, the angle normaliser's
-- [0, 360) range, the eccentricity bound, and the sexagesimal rendering.
--
-- The two that do not are `vec_norm` and `declination`.  Vera's float builtins
-- are opaque to the SMT solver by design (SKILL.md), so a claim standing on the
-- far side of a `sqrt` or an `asin` cannot be proved and is enforced by a
-- runtime guard at the function boundary instead.
--
-- Compare `right_ascension` and `declination` directly.  Both assert a range on
-- an angle, both are true by construction, and both are the same shape of claim.
-- `right_ascension` PROVES, because its value returns through `wrap_deg`, whose
-- own range is proved, so the chain of evidence is unbroken.  `declination` does
-- not, because `asin` sits between the claim and the evidence.  Nothing about
-- the difficulty of the mathematics separates them; only whether a
-- transcendental intervenes.  That is the tier system doing exactly what it is
-- specified to do, made visible in a program that does real work.
--
-- The formatting layer takes plain `@Float64` rather than the refined types the
-- computation carries, because a refinement widens to its base for free and the
-- renderer has no use for the constraint.  Its output is built with explicit
-- `float_to_string` / `int_to_string` and `string_concat`, which is a style
-- choice rather than a limitation: interpolation renders a refined value
-- exactly as the primitive it refines.
--
-- =============================================================================
-- ACCURACY
-- =============================================================================
--
-- The elements are JPL's low-precision Keplerian set ("Approximate Positions of
-- the Major Planets"), valid 1800–2050.  Against ERFA's analytic model this
-- program agrees to 19 arcsec for the Sun and 5 arcsec for Mars — well inside
-- the arcminute accuracy the element set claims.
--
-- Two simplifications are stated rather than hidden.  TT and UTC are conflated
-- (a ~69 s difference, far below the element set's accuracy), and no correction
-- is made for light-time, aberration, nutation, or precession from J2000.
-- =============================================================================

-- Distinct aliases partition the slot index space.  See the header.
type Julian = Float64;  -- Julian Day number

type Century = Float64;  -- Julian centuries from J2000.0

type Deg = Float64;  -- angle, degrees

type Rad = Float64;  -- angle, radians

type SemiMajor = Float64;  -- semi-major axis, AU

type Incl = Float64;  -- inclination, degrees

type MeanAnom = Float64;  -- mean anomaly, degrees

type EccAnom = Float64;  -- eccentric anomaly, degrees

type ArgPeri = Float64;  -- argument of perihelion, degrees

type LongNode = Float64;  -- longitude of ascending node, degrees

type OrbX = Float64;  -- in-plane coordinate, AU

type OrbY = Float64;  -- in-plane coordinate, AU

type XAu = Float64;  -- ecliptic/equatorial X, AU

type YAu = Float64;  -- ecliptic/equatorial Y, AU

type ZAu = Float64;  -- ecliptic/equatorial Z, AU

-- Refinement, not a plain alias: the bound is the physics.  An eccentricity of
-- 1 or more is not an ellipse, and `kepler_step` divides by (1 - e*cos E).
type Ecc = { @Float64 | @Float64.0 >= 0.0 && @Float64.0 < 1.0 };

private data Vec3 {
  Vec3(XAu, YAu, ZAu)
}

-- Orbital elements already propagated to a given epoch.  Six fields, six types,
-- so a destructure yields six independent stacks rather than one six-deep one.
private data Elements {
  Elements(SemiMajor, Ecc, Incl, MeanAnom, ArgPeri, LongNode)
}

-- =============================================================================
-- Angles
-- =============================================================================

private fn deg_to_rad(@Deg -> @Rad)
  requires(true)
  ensures(true)
  effects(pure)
{
  @Deg.0 * 0.017453292519943295
}

-- Normalise an angle into [0, 360).  The postcondition is PROVED, not checked:
-- every branch either returns a value already shown to be in range, or recurses
-- on one closer to it, and the fuel-exhausted branch returns a constant that is.
-- Angle-wrap bugs are a classic source of silent error in pointing and
-- navigation code; here the compiler rules one out for all inputs.
private fn wrap_deg(@Deg, @Nat -> @Deg)
  requires(true)
  ensures(@Deg.result >= 0.0 && @Deg.result < 360.0)
  decreases(@Nat.0)
  effects(pure)
{
  if @Deg.0 >= 0.0 && @Deg.0 < 360.0 then {
    @Deg.0
  } else {
    if @Nat.0 == 0 then {
      0.0
    } else {
      if @Deg.0 >= 360.0 then {
        wrap_deg(@Deg.0 - 360.0, @Nat.0 - 1)
      } else {
        wrap_deg(@Deg.0 + 360.0, @Nat.0 - 1)
      }
    }
  }
}

-- =============================================================================
-- Kepler's equation:  M = E - e sin E
-- =============================================================================

-- One Newton-Raphson step, in degrees.  Three parameters, three stacks, every
-- reference at `.0`.  The two `let` bindings are `Rad` and `Deg` — deliberately
-- different types, so neither shifts the other's index.
private fn kepler_step(@EccAnom, @Ecc, @MeanAnom -> @EccAnom)
  requires(true)
  ensures(true)
  effects(pure)
{
  let @Rad = deg_to_rad(@EccAnom.0);
  let @Deg = @MeanAnom.0 - (@EccAnom.0 - 57.29577951308232 * @Ecc.0 * sin(@Rad.0));
  @EccAnom.0 + @Deg.0 / (1.0 - @Ecc.0 * cos(@Rad.0))
}

-- Iterate to convergence.  `decreases` on the fuel counter is a termination
-- proof: this loop cannot run forever, whatever the solver does numerically.
private fn kepler_solve(@EccAnom, @Ecc, @MeanAnom, @Nat -> @EccAnom)
  requires(true)
  ensures(true)
  decreases(@Nat.0)
  effects(pure)
{
  if @Nat.0 == 0 then {
    @EccAnom.0
  } else {
    kepler_solve(kepler_step(@EccAnom.0, @Ecc.0, @MeanAnom.0), @Ecc.0, @MeanAnom.0, @Nat.0 - 1)
  }
}

-- =============================================================================
-- Orbital elements, propagated linearly from J2000.0
-- =============================================================================

private fn julian_century(@Julian -> @Century)
  requires(true)
  ensures(true)
  effects(pure)
{
  (@Julian.0 - 2451545.0) / 36525.0
}

private fn earth_elements(@Century -> @Elements)
  requires(true)
  ensures(true)
  effects(pure)
{
  let @Deg = wrap_deg(100.46457166 + 35999.37244981 * @Century.0 - (102.93768193 + 0.32327364 * @Century.0), 400);
  -- The eccentricity is bounded at construction rather than inherited from a
  -- refined date type.  The guard condition is EXACTLY the `Ecc` predicate, so
  -- the then-branch discharges by path condition and the else-branch by
  -- constant folding -- the shape `E505`'s own diagnostic recommends.  NaN
  -- takes the else branch (`NaN >= 0.0` is false), which is the case a bare
  -- `float_clamp` cannot cover: WASM min/max propagate NaN, so a clamped value
  -- still fails an ellipse bound the solver must prove.
  -- Earth's e stays within a few parts in 10^5 of 0.0167 across 1800-2050, so
  -- the fallback never fires for a date this element set covers.
  let @Float64 = 0.01671123 - 0.00004392 * @Century.0;
  let @Ecc = if @Float64.0 >= 0.0 && @Float64.0 < 1.0 then {
    @Float64.0
  } else {
    0.01671123
  };
  Elements(1.00000261 + 0.00000562 * @Century.0, @Ecc.0, -0.00001531 - 0.01294668 * @Century.0, @Deg.0, 102.93768193 + 0.32327364 * @Century.0, 0.0)
}

private fn mars_elements(@Century -> @Elements)
  requires(true)
  ensures(true)
  effects(pure)
{
  let @Deg = wrap_deg(-4.55343205 + 19140.30268499 * @Century.0 - (-23.94362959 + 0.44441088 * @Century.0), 400);
  -- The eccentricity is bounded at construction rather than inherited from a
  -- refined date type.  The guard condition is EXACTLY the `Ecc` predicate, so
  -- the then-branch discharges by path condition and the else-branch by
  -- constant folding -- the shape `E505`'s own diagnostic recommends.  NaN
  -- takes the else branch (`NaN >= 0.0` is false), which is the case a bare
  -- `float_clamp` cannot cover: WASM min/max propagate NaN, so a clamped value
  -- still fails an ellipse bound the solver must prove.
  -- Mars's e stays within a few parts in 10^5 of 0.0934 across the same span.
  let @Float64 = 0.0933941 + 0.00007882 * @Century.0;
  let @Ecc = if @Float64.0 >= 0.0 && @Float64.0 < 1.0 then {
    @Float64.0
  } else {
    0.0933941
  };
  Elements(1.52371034 + 0.00001847 * @Century.0, @Ecc.0, 1.84969142 - 0.00813131 * @Century.0, @Deg.0, -23.94362959 + 0.44441088 * @Century.0, 49.55953891 - 0.29257343 * @Century.0)
}

-- =============================================================================
-- Orbital plane to J2000 ecliptic
-- =============================================================================
--
-- The rotation is three separate functions rather than one returning a triple.
-- Each takes the two in-plane coordinates and the three orientation angles: five
-- parameters, five distinct types, so every reference inside is `.0`.  Written
-- with five bare `@Float64` parameters these bodies would be a wall of
-- `@Float64.4 * @Float64.2 - @Float64.3 * @Float64.1`, and a transposition
-- would typecheck cleanly and give a wrong sky position.

private fn rot_x(@OrbX, @OrbY, @ArgPeri, @Incl, @LongNode -> @XAu)
  requires(true)
  ensures(true)
  effects(pure)
{
  let @Rad = deg_to_rad(@ArgPeri.0);
  (cos(@Rad.0) * cos(deg_to_rad(@LongNode.0)) - sin(@Rad.0) * sin(deg_to_rad(@LongNode.0)) * cos(deg_to_rad(@Incl.0))) * @OrbX.0 + (0.0 - sin(@Rad.0) * cos(deg_to_rad(@LongNode.0)) - cos(@Rad.0) * sin(deg_to_rad(@LongNode.0)) * cos(deg_to_rad(@Incl.0))) * @OrbY.0
}

private fn rot_y(@OrbX, @OrbY, @ArgPeri, @Incl, @LongNode -> @YAu)
  requires(true)
  ensures(true)
  effects(pure)
{
  let @Rad = deg_to_rad(@ArgPeri.0);
  (cos(@Rad.0) * sin(deg_to_rad(@LongNode.0)) + sin(@Rad.0) * cos(deg_to_rad(@LongNode.0)) * cos(deg_to_rad(@Incl.0))) * @OrbX.0 + (0.0 - sin(@Rad.0) * sin(deg_to_rad(@LongNode.0)) + cos(@Rad.0) * cos(deg_to_rad(@LongNode.0)) * cos(deg_to_rad(@Incl.0))) * @OrbY.0
}

private fn rot_z(@OrbX, @OrbY, @ArgPeri, @Incl -> @ZAu)
  requires(true)
  ensures(true)
  effects(pure)
{
  let @Rad = deg_to_rad(@ArgPeri.0);
  sin(@Rad.0) * sin(deg_to_rad(@Incl.0)) * @OrbX.0 + cos(@Rad.0) * sin(deg_to_rad(@Incl.0)) * @OrbY.0
}

-- Heliocentric position, J2000 ecliptic frame.
private fn helio(@Elements -> @Vec3)
  requires(true)
  ensures(true)
  effects(pure)
{
  match @Elements.0 {
    Elements(@SemiMajor, @Ecc, @Incl, @MeanAnom, @ArgPeri, @LongNode) -> {
      let @EccAnom = kepler_solve(@MeanAnom.0, @Ecc.0, @MeanAnom.0, 40);
      let @Rad = deg_to_rad(@EccAnom.0);
      let @OrbX = @SemiMajor.0 * (cos(@Rad.0) - @Ecc.0);
      let @OrbY = @SemiMajor.0 * sqrt(1.0 - @Ecc.0 * @Ecc.0) * sin(@Rad.0);
      let @ArgPeri = @ArgPeri.0 - @LongNode.0;
      Vec3(rot_x(@OrbX.0, @OrbY.0, @ArgPeri.0, @Incl.0, @LongNode.0), rot_y(@OrbX.0, @OrbY.0, @ArgPeri.0, @Incl.0, @LongNode.0), rot_z(@OrbX.0, @OrbY.0, @ArgPeri.0, @Incl.0))
    }
  }
}

-- =============================================================================
-- Geocentric, then equatorial
-- =============================================================================

-- Nested destructure: the OUTER match's bindings sit at `.1` once the inner
-- match pushes its own.  `@XAu.1 - @XAu.0` is therefore first minus second.
private fn vec_sub(@Vec3, @Vec3 -> @Vec3)
  requires(true)
  ensures(true)
  effects(pure)
{
  match @Vec3.1 {
    Vec3(@XAu, @YAu, @ZAu) -> match @Vec3.0 {
      Vec3(@XAu, @YAu, @ZAu) -> Vec3(@XAu.1 - @XAu.0, @YAu.1 - @YAu.0, @ZAu.1 - @ZAu.0)
    }
  }
}

-- Rotate the ecliptic frame onto the equator (obliquity at J2000).
private fn to_equatorial(@Vec3 -> @Vec3)
  requires(true)
  ensures(true)
  effects(pure)
{
  match @Vec3.0 {
    Vec3(@XAu, @YAu, @ZAu) -> {
      let @Rad = deg_to_rad(23.43928);
      Vec3(@XAu.0, @YAu.0 * cos(@Rad.0) - @ZAu.0 * sin(@Rad.0), @YAu.0 * sin(@Rad.0) + @ZAu.0 * cos(@Rad.0))
    }
  }
}

-- A distance cannot be negative.  `sqrt` is a float builtin, so this is a
-- Tier 3 runtime check rather than a proof -- the boundary in action.
private fn vec_norm(@Vec3 -> @Float64)
  requires(true)
  ensures(@Float64.result >= 0.0)
  effects(pure)
{
  match @Vec3.0 {
    Vec3(@XAu, @YAu, @ZAu) -> sqrt(@XAu.0 * @XAu.0 + @YAu.0 * @YAu.0 + @ZAu.0 * @ZAu.0)
  }
}

-- Contrast with `declination`: this postcondition is identical in spirit but
-- PROVES, because the value comes back through `wrap_deg` whose own range is
-- proved.  Same file, same kind of claim, different tier -- the difference is
-- whether a transcendental sits between the claim and the evidence.
private fn right_ascension(@Vec3 -> @Deg)
  requires(true)
  ensures(@Deg.result >= 0.0 && @Deg.result < 360.0)
  effects(pure)
{
  match @Vec3.0 {
    Vec3(@XAu, @YAu, @ZAu) -> {
      let @Deg = atan2(@YAu.0, @XAu.0) * 57.29577951308232;
      wrap_deg(@Deg.0, 4)
    }
  }
}

-- Declination is an angle off the equator, so it lies in [-90, 90].  True by
-- construction (`asin` cannot return more), but `asin` is opaque to the solver,
-- so the guarantee is enforced at run time instead of proved.
private fn declination(@Vec3 -> @Deg)
  requires(true)
  ensures(@Deg.result >= -90.0 && @Deg.result <= 90.0)
  effects(pure)
{
  match @Vec3.0 {
    Vec3(@XAu, @YAu, @ZAu) -> asin(@ZAu.0 / vec_norm(Vec3(@XAu.0, @YAu.0, @ZAu.0))) * 57.29577951308232
  }
}

-- =============================================================================
-- Sexagesimal rendering — back on Tier 1 ground
-- =============================================================================

private fn pad2(@Int -> @String)
  requires(true)
  ensures(true)
  effects(pure)
{
  string_pad_start(int_to_string(@Int.0), 2, "0")
}

-- Right ascension as hours, minutes and seconds to one decimal place.
private fn format_ra(@Deg -> @String)
  requires(true)
  ensures(true)
  effects(pure)
{
  let @Float64 = @Deg.0 / 15.0;
  let @Int = floor(@Float64.0);
  let @Float64 = (@Float64.0 - int_to_float(@Int.0)) * 60.0;
  let @Int = floor(@Float64.0);
  let @Float64 = (@Float64.0 - int_to_float(@Int.0)) * 600.0;
  string_concat(string_concat(pad2(floor(@Deg.0 / 15.0)), "h"), string_concat(string_concat(pad2(@Int.0), "m"), string_concat(string_concat(pad2(round(@Float64.0) / 10), "."), string_concat(int_to_string(round(@Float64.0) % 10), "s"))))
}

-- Declination as signed degrees, arcminutes and arcseconds.
private fn format_dec(@Deg -> @String)
  requires(true)
  ensures(true)
  effects(pure)
{
  let @String = if @Deg.0 < 0.0 then {
    "-"
  } else {
    "+"
  };
  let @Float64 = if @Deg.0 < 0.0 then {
    0.0 - @Deg.0
  } else {
    @Deg.0
  };
  let @Int = floor(@Float64.0);
  let @Float64 = (@Float64.0 - int_to_float(@Int.0)) * 60.0;
  string_concat(string_concat(@String.0, string_concat(pad2(@Int.0), "d")), string_concat(string_concat(pad2(floor(@Float64.0)), "m"), string_concat(pad2(round((@Float64.0 - int_to_float(floor(@Float64.0))) * 60.0)), "s")))
}

private fn report(@String, @Vec3 -> @String)
  requires(true)
  ensures(true)
  effects(pure)
{
  string_concat(string_concat("  ", string_concat(string_pad_end(@String.0, 7, " "), "RA ")), string_concat(string_concat(format_ra(right_ascension(@Vec3.0)), "   Dec "), string_concat(string_concat(format_dec(declination(@Vec3.0)), "   "), string_concat(float_to_string(vec_norm(@Vec3.0)), " AU"))))
}

-- =============================================================================

public fn main(@Unit -> @Unit)
  requires(true)
  ensures(true)
  effects(<IO>)
{
  let @Century = julian_century(2461456.5);
  let @Vec3 = helio(earth_elements(@Century.0));
  let @Vec3 = helio(mars_elements(@Century.0));

  -- The geocentric Sun is Earth's heliocentric position reversed.
  let @Vec3 = to_equatorial(vec_sub(Vec3(0.0, 0.0, 0.0), @Vec3.1));
  -- Mars needs the real two-body subtraction.
  let @Vec3 = to_equatorial(vec_sub(@Vec3.1, @Vec3.2));

  IO.print("Geocentric positions for JD 2461456.5 (2027 February 20.0 TT)\n\n");
  IO.print(string_concat(report("Sun", @Vec3.1), "\n"));
  IO.print(string_concat(report("Mars", @Vec3.0), "\n"))
}
